1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,632
The use case itself: agents answering policy questions by searching several systems

2
00:00:05,632 --> 00:00:06,141
by hand.

3
00:00:06,141 --> 00:00:11,295
Open A06 from last lecture, and give this the same twenty minutes Section three's

4
00:00:11,295 --> 00:00:12,377
PRD workshop did.

5
00:00:12,377 --> 00:00:15,813
Can you finish all six sections honestly in that time?

6
00:00:15,813 --> 00:00:17,659
Not perfectly, just honestly.

7
00:00:17,659 --> 00:00:22,813
Every section needs something in it, even where that something is a number you'll

8
00:00:22,813 --> 00:00:23,768
validate later.

9
00:00:23,768 --> 00:00:28,859
Support is the right use case to practise on precisely because it's the one this

10
00:00:28,859 --> 00:00:34,140
course has walked through from every angle already: the archetype from Section one,

11
00:00:34,140 --> 00:00:39,231
the threshold from Section three, and now the retrieval mechanics from this one.

12
00:00:39,231 --> 00:00:44,449
Designing it end to end here is what turns six separate lectures into one document

13
00:00:44,449 --> 00:00:46,549
you actually know how to produce.

14
00:00:46,549 --> 00:00:51,894
Skip the exercise, and those six lectures stay six separate ideas in your head, ones

15
00:00:51,894 --> 00:00:56,985
you can discuss in a meeting but have never actually tested against a page you'd

16
00:00:56,985 --> 00:00:58,258
hand to an engineer.

17
00:00:58,808 --> 00:01:03,708
Notice why RAG gets the same five minutes acceptance criteria got in Section three's

18
00:01:03,708 --> 00:01:08,433
workshop, and for the identical reason: everything after it gets easier once that

19
00:01:08,433 --> 00:01:10,708
argument is actually made, not assumed.

20
00:01:10,708 --> 00:01:15,258
For the support case specifically, that argument is usually straightforward to

21
00:01:15,258 --> 00:01:15,608
write.

22
00:01:15,608 --> 00:01:20,333
Agents are searching policy documents by hand today, the policies are private and

23
00:01:20,333 --> 00:01:24,941
change quarterly, and no amount of prompting supplies information the model was

24
00:01:24,941 --> 00:01:25,641
never given.

25
00:01:25,641 --> 00:01:30,483
If your own case doesn't have an argument that clean, that's worth noticing now, in

26
00:01:30,483 --> 00:01:32,467
minute five, not in minute twenty.

27
00:01:32,467 --> 00:01:36,958
The pipeline stages minutes go quickly for the same use case, because support

28
00:01:36,958 --> 00:01:40,050
content rarely needs the full seven stages built out.

29
00:01:40,050 --> 00:01:44,775
Most support corpora don't need query rewriting or a heavy re-ranking pass on day

30
00:01:44,775 --> 00:01:45,008
one.

31
00:01:45,008 --> 00:01:49,675
Naming what you're deliberately leaving out is as much a decision as naming what

32
00:01:49,675 --> 00:01:53,117
you're building, and it belongs in the document either way.

33
00:01:53,667 --> 00:01:58,750
Chunking strategy and retrieval targets share the middle eight minutes, and neither

34
00:01:58,750 --> 00:02:00,833
has to be perfect on a first pass.

35
00:02:00,833 --> 00:02:05,916
Sorting each content type into its own natural chunk, an FAQ pair, a policy clause,

36
00:02:05,916 --> 00:02:10,816
a full ticket thread, usually goes faster than people expect with Section five's

37
00:02:10,816 --> 00:02:12,102
examples still fresh.

38
00:02:12,102 --> 00:02:17,247
The number that actually eats the full four minutes is the retrieval target, because

39
00:02:17,247 --> 00:02:21,840
it's the first point in the exercise where you're committing to something a

40
00:02:21,840 --> 00:02:25,086
colleague could check against a real scorecard later.

41
00:02:25,086 --> 00:02:30,047
The last four minutes, failure coverage and permissions, go fastest if you resist

42
00:02:30,047 --> 00:02:32,803
listing every failure from two lectures back.

43
00:02:32,803 --> 00:02:37,887
Pick the two failures most likely to actually bite a support corpus: near-duplicate

44
00:02:37,887 --> 00:02:42,909
confusion from old tickets nobody archived, and permission leakage from restricted

45
00:02:42,909 --> 00:02:46,217
policy pages sitting in the same index as public ones.

46
00:02:46,217 --> 00:02:50,994
Two named failures with real fixes beat a generic list copied straight off the

47
00:02:50,994 --> 00:02:51,361
table.

48
00:02:51,911 --> 00:02:53,333
Recognise this sentence.

49
00:02:53,333 --> 00:02:57,835
It's the exact worked example from last lecture, and seeing it again here is

50
00:02:57,835 --> 00:03:02,811
deliberate: this is roughly what your own retrieval targets and permissions sections

51
00:03:02,811 --> 00:03:06,010
should sound like once you've filled them in for real.

52
00:03:06,010 --> 00:03:10,867
If what you've written in the last few minutes doesn't have this shape, a scope, a

53
00:03:10,867 --> 00:03:15,725
chunking approach, two numbers, a permission model, a re-index cadence, that's the

54
00:03:15,725 --> 00:03:20,346
gap worth closing while you're still in the section, not three sections later.

55
00:03:20,346 --> 00:03:25,144
Notice, too, what this sentence deliberately doesn't include: no mention of which

56
00:03:25,144 --> 00:03:27,810
vector database, no model name, no framework.

57
00:03:27,810 --> 00:03:30,712
None of that belongs in a design doc a PM writes.

58
00:03:30,712 --> 00:03:33,970
This sentence specifies what the system has to achieve.

59
00:03:33,970 --> 00:03:38,887
It leaves precisely how to engineering, the same boundary Section three drew around

60
00:03:38,887 --> 00:03:39,894
the PRD template.

61
00:03:39,894 --> 00:03:44,811
It took this section six lectures to earn a sentence that specific, so don't expect

62
00:03:44,811 --> 00:03:47,062
your own version on the first attempt.

63
00:03:47,062 --> 00:03:51,979
A rougher draft that still names a scope, a chunking approach, and two numbers is a

64
00:03:51,979 --> 00:03:56,658
completely reasonable place to be at the sixteen minute mark, with four minutes

65
00:03:56,658 --> 00:03:58,139
still left to sharpen it.

66
00:03:58,689 --> 00:04:03,682
Say the honest caveat, because this is the section most people stall on, and for a

67
00:04:03,682 --> 00:04:08,735
reasonable reason: you genuinely don't know your real hit rate until you've run the

68
00:04:08,735 --> 00:04:11,597
scorecard from 5.4, and you haven't run it yet.

69
00:04:11,597 --> 00:04:13,058
Write the number anyway.

70
00:04:13,058 --> 00:04:18,172
Ninety percent hit rate, seventy percent precision, whatever feels like a defensible

71
00:04:18,172 --> 00:04:22,860
starting bar for this specific use case, and mark it "target, to be validated

72
00:04:22,860 --> 00:04:27,670
against the scorecard." That's not a weaker answer than a real measured number.

73
00:04:27,670 --> 00:04:32,723
It's the honest, correctly-labelled version of exactly where you are right now, and

74
00:04:32,723 --> 00:04:37,837
it's infinitely more useful than a blank cell nobody will ever come back to fill in.

75
00:04:37,837 --> 00:04:42,769
This is the same move as the placeholder threshold from Section three's workshop,

76
00:04:42,769 --> 00:04:45,265
applied one section later in the process.

77
00:04:45,265 --> 00:04:49,953
A guessed number, clearly labelled as a guess, is a real decision that can be

78
00:04:49,953 --> 00:04:50,440
revised.

79
00:04:50,440 --> 00:04:55,311
A blank field is a decision nobody made yet, quietly waiting to become someone's

80
00:04:55,311 --> 00:04:59,268
problem during launch week instead of during this twenty minutes.

81
00:04:59,268 --> 00:05:01,216
A labelled guess also ages well.

82
00:05:01,216 --> 00:05:06,087
Six months from now, whoever reopens this document can see exactly which numbers

83
00:05:06,087 --> 00:05:11,080
were real and which were placeholders, instead of wondering whether ninety percent

84
00:05:11,080 --> 00:05:13,637
was ever actually tested against anything.

85
00:05:14,187 --> 00:05:18,713
That's today's bar, stated plainly: attempted, not perfected, and every number

86
00:05:18,713 --> 00:05:21,092
labelled honestly as measured or guessed.

87
00:05:21,092 --> 00:05:25,792
Six sections in twenty minutes averages under four minutes each, which is exactly

88
00:05:25,792 --> 00:05:28,925
why a labelled guess beats stalling on any single one.

89
00:05:28,925 --> 00:05:32,639
If you finish with time left, don't smooth every section evenly.

90
00:05:32,639 --> 00:05:37,339
Return to why RAG, the same instruction as Section three's workshop, because it's

91
00:05:37,339 --> 00:05:40,704
still the section the other five are quietly depending on.

92
00:05:41,254 --> 00:05:42,950
One thing before you move on.

93
00:05:42,950 --> 00:05:47,861
Finish the six sections, and anywhere you guessed a number, say so explicitly rather

94
00:05:47,861 --> 00:05:50,493
than letting it look more certain than it is.

95
00:05:50,493 --> 00:05:55,054
Then send it to whoever actually owns the support content, today, and ask what

96
00:05:55,054 --> 00:05:56,574
they'd push back on first.

97
00:05:56,574 --> 00:06:01,369
They'll usually find the gap in minutes that would have taken you a week to notice

98
00:06:01,369 --> 00:06:01,719
alone.

99
00:06:01,719 --> 00:06:05,579
Expect their first question to be about permissions, not chunking.

100
00:06:05,579 --> 00:06:10,198
Content owners live inside the access model every day, and they'll notice a gap

101
00:06:10,198 --> 00:06:14,934
there faster than anyone else in the room, before you've even finished explaining

102
00:06:14,934 --> 00:06:16,396
the rest of the document.

103
00:06:16,396 --> 00:06:21,191
Next lecture closes out this section: what you've actually built, and how directly

104
00:06:21,191 --> 00:06:25,986
it plugs into Section six, where retrieval meets action and the whole conversation

105
00:06:25,986 --> 00:06:26,453
changes.
